# ckatsak, Mon Jun  8 07:34:45 PM EEST 2026
#
# This file is part of snaplace.
#
# Optionally place this under `/etc/udev/rules.d/`, to instruct udev not to run
# hotplug helper programs for all TAP devices matching the `uvm-*` name prefix.
#
# snaplace/FaaSCell creates TAP devices in bulk, e.g., when bootstrapping and
# populating its TAP pool.  On systemd-based hosts, udev may run helper
# programs for every newly-created network interface.  In particular, systemd
# ships a rule that runs `systemd-sysctl` for each new non-loopback network
# device, roughly as: `--prefix=/net/ipv{4,6}/{conf,neigh}/$name`.
# This applies per-interface sysctl settings under paths such as:
# - `net.ipv4.conf..*`
# - `net.ipv4.neigh..*`
# - `net.ipv6.conf..*`
# - `net.ipv6.neigh..*`
#
# While this might be reasonable for physical and/or host-managed interfaces,
# snaplace-created `uvm-*` TAP devices are per-sandbox implementation details
# owned by snaplace itself.  Running one or more udev hotplug helpers per TAP
# creates CPU churn (at high TAP creation/removal rates), and does not provide
# useful configuration for these devices.
#
# This rule replaces udev's accumulated `RUN` list for matching devices with a
# final no-op helper:
# - `KERNEL=="uvm-*"` matches snaplace-created TAP devices by their kernel
#   device name;
# - `ACTION=="add|remove"` covers both TAP creation and removal churn;
# - `RUN{program}:="/usr/bin/true"` finally assigns this no-op binary as the
#   list of udev helper programs, so any helper appended by earlier rules (such
#   as `systemd-sysctl`) are not executed for these devices.  (Note that this
#   still incurs the fork+exec cost; unfortunately, an empty list is disallowed
#   by `udevadm verify`, so `/usr/bin/true` is probably the best alternative.)
#
# The `zy-` prefix is intentional.  udev loads rules from multiple directories
# and sorts them lexicographically; later matching rules can still append to
# list-valued keys such as `RUN`.  Therefore, this file must sort after the
# distribution/systemd rules that append hotplug helpers, otherwise clearing
# `RUN` would happen too early and a later rule could append another helper.
#
# This file is useful/needed when:
# - the host's udev rules run expensive or unnecessary `RUN+=...` helper
#   programs for `uvm-*` TAP devices; for example, per-interface
#   `systemd-sysctl` invocations; and
# - snaplace creates/removes TAP devices at high enough rates for this churn
#   to be measurable.
#
# This file is unnecessary when:
# - no udev rule appends `RUN+=...` helper programs for `uvm-*` devices; or
# - TAP creation/removal is sufficiently rare that the helper overhead is
#   irrelevant; or
# - the host has already been configured with an equivalent later udev rule
#   that prevents hotplug helpers from running for `uvm-*`.
#
# This file does not replace `10-snaplace.link`.  The `.link` file is consumed
# by `systemd-udevd` (net_setup_link`) and prevents systemd's link setup policy
# from rewriting the TAP MAC address.  This udev rule replaces only the `RUN`
# helper-program list.  They operate on different parts of the same udev event
# processing path and may coexist.
#
# This file also does not replace `01-snaplace-uvm-unmanaged.network`.  The
# `.network` file is consumed by `systemd-networkd` and prevents networkd from
# managing/configuring `uvm-*` devices.  This udev rule prevents udev helper
# programs, such as `systemd-sysctl`, from being spawned for them.  They
# address different sources of host-side interference/churn and may coexist.
#
# On ifupdown-based hosts, this file may overlap with `80-ifupdown.rules` in
# purpose but not in exact mechanism.  `80-ifupdown.rules` prevents the
# ifupdown hotplug path from running for `uvm-*` while preserving it for other
# interfaces.  This file is broader: it replaces the entire udev `RUN`
# helper-program list for `uvm-*`.  Use both only if both classes of churn
# exist and the broader behavior is intended.
#
# To reload udev rules:
#
#   # udevadm control --reload-rules
#
# References:
# - udev(7)
# - sysctl.d(5)
# - systemd-sysctl.service(8)

SUBSYSTEM=="net", ACTION=="add|remove", KERNEL=="uvm-*", RUN{program}:="/usr/bin/true"
